.TH DUMPEXE 1 "October 2026" "RetroCodeMess" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dumpexe \- MS-DOS / Win16 binary analyzer: MZ EXE, NE, .COM, and .SYS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dumpexe
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B dumpexe
.RB [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.br
.B dumpexe
.RB [ options ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dumpexe
is a comprehensive utility for analyzing 16-bit MS-DOS and Windows 3.x binaries.
It supports MZ EXE, Windows 3.x NE (New Executable), plain .COM programs,
and DOS device driver (.SYS) files — and provides TDUMP-style output with
multiple analysis modes:
.IP \(bu 2
Static header analysis with detailed field information
.IP \(bu 2
Relocation table display with padding analysis (MZ EXE)
.IP \(bu 2
Hex+ASCII dumps with zero-compression (like hexdump -C)
.IP \(bu 2
x86-16 disassembly using Capstone (static code analysis)
.IP \(bu 2
DOS load simulation with register tracking (dynamic execution trace)
.IP \(bu 2
NE header, segment table, imports, and resource table (Win16)
.PP
The file format is detected automatically from the file content:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B MZ EXE
\(em first two bytes are
.B 4Dh 5Ah
('MZ'); pure DOS load image (no NE at e_lfanew)
.IP \(bu 2
.B NE EXE (Windows 3.x / Win16)
\(em MZ stub with
.B e_lfanew
at offset 3Ch pointing to
.B 4Eh 45h
('NE'). Dumps segments, imports, resources;
.B \-d
disassembles the entry code segment and stops at that segment's on-disk end.
.IP \(bu 2
.B .SYS device driver
\(em first four bytes are
.B FFFFFFFFh
.IP \(bu 2
.B .COM (plain binary)
\(em fallback for all other DOS binaries
.RE

.SH OPTIONS
.SS General Options
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit. Also shown when no arguments are provided.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.

.SS Display Sections
If no section options are given, the file header information is displayed, and for MZ EXE and .COM binaries a default 64-byte hex+ASCII preview of the entry point is also shown.
Multiple options can be combined.

.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-relocation
Show relocation table with all entries, including header padding before and after the table.
.B MZ EXE only.
The relocation table contains segment fixup locations that DOS applies when loading the program.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-hexdump
Show complete hex+ASCII dump from entry point to end of file.
Uses canonical hexdump -C format with zero-compression (repeated lines shown as *).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-disassemble
Show x86-16 disassembly from entry point to end of file using Capstone.
This is
.B static analysis
\- shows all instructions without execution.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Show all sections in order: static info, relocation table (EXE), hexdump, and disassembly.
Also unpacks the same packers as \-d.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no-int-annotations
Suppress INT annotation comments in disassembly output.
.TP
.B \-\-strings
Extract Pascal length-prefixed, CALL-inline, and ASCIIZ strings (opt-in; default off).
.TP
.B \-\-no-pascal-mt
Disable Pascal MT+86 3.1.1 detection and annotation.
.B Default: on.
When signatures match, dumpexe prints compiler ID, entry CALL + segment table,
near-JMP procedure vector table, and INIPC/PASLIB fingerprint hits.
Only a disable switch is provided (sane-defaults CLI policy).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a machine-readable JSON report on stdout (default: off).
Includes MZ header, Pascal MT+ report, strings, and CFG summary.
Human text sections are suppressed.
Does not unpack.
Combine with
.B \-\-cfg-dot
to also write a Graphviz file (path recorded in JSON).
.TP
.B \-\-cfg-dot=FILE
Write a Graphviz DOT digraph of the static CFG to FILE (default: off).
Nodes are colored by role (entry, interesting, jump-table, call target).
Does not imply a full human CFG dump unless
.B \-\-cfg
is also given. Render with e.g.
.BR "dot -Tpng FILE -o cfg.png" .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-disassemble
Emit a
.B multi-pass
annotated assembly listing (not a single Capstone stream only):
provisional
.B func_<IP>
labels, INT annotations, near
.B call
/
.B jmp
targets rewritten to labels, blank lines after procedure regions.
There is
.B no
.B \-\-listing
flag; this
.I is
the listing mode.
Also writes
.I <stem>.asm
next to the input by default.
Microsoft EXEPACK, LZEXE 0.91, LZEXE 0.90, PKLITE, DIET, and LHarc also write
.I <stem>_UNPACKED.EXE
(or
.I .COM
when the image is not MZ) and
.I <stem>_UNPACKED.asm
of the unpacked program.
.B \-o
names only the packed listing.
DIET matches Deark
.B identify_diet_fmt
(EXE stub at
.I codestart\-32+{77,72,52,55}
plus the COM and data patterns).
The four bytes
.B DIET
at file offset 1Ch are not enough.
LHarc matches an embedded header that would pass Deark
.B de_identify_lha
and also has a level-0/1 header checksum or a level-2 CRC, a packed
size that fits, and a start in the SFX trailer (the end of the MZ image).
An
.B LHA
sentence is not enough.
A checksummed header in the middle of the file is not enough.
LZEXE 0.91 or 0.90 suppresses LHarc.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " PATH"
Override the listing output path (default:
.IR <stem>.asm ).
.B \-\-output=PATH
is the same switch.
Use
.B \-
for stdout only.
.B \-o \-
writes no
.IR <stem>.asm ,
no
.IR <stem>_UNPACKED.EXE ,
and no
.IR <stem>_UNPACKED.asm .
Both listings still go to stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-no-asm-file
Do not write an
.B .asm
file (default when disassembling is to write one).
With
.B \-\-uasm
and no
.BR \-o ,
this is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-uasm
Write UASM source for
.BR uasm (1)
(v2.57) to
.I <stem>.asm
or to the path named by
.BR \-o .
This switch is enable-only and does not require
.BR \-d .
The UASM file has no address column and no hex-byte column: one instruction
per line (mnemonic and operands only), or
.B db
of the exact bytes.
Opcode 98 is printed
.B cbw
and opcode 99 is printed
.B cwd .
A relative branch is
.B jmp short ,
.B jmp near ptr ,
.B call near ptr ,
or a conditional
.B short
when the target is a label.
Any other non-branch line is kept only when
.BR uasm (1)
assembles it back to those bytes.
A relocated
.B "mov r16, imm16"
(opcodes B8 through BF) whose paragraph frame lies in this image is printed as
.B "mov rx, (dxfrm_XXXX - dximg0) SHR 4"
and is not sent through that one-instruction check.
Ordinary
.B \-d
/
.B \-a
stdout keeps the address listing
.RI ( "0100  B8004C  mov ..." )
when the UASM text is written to a file.
.B \-d
or
.B \-a
together with this switch and a real
.B \-o
path prints that address listing and writes UASM to the file.
.B \-o \-
writes UASM to stdout and no file. With
.B \-\-uasm
that stdout is the UASM source only: no address listing, including when
.B \-d
or
.B \-a
is also set.
.B .186
/
.B .286
/
.B .386
are emitted after
.B .model
so a stood-behind 16-bit instruction is not assembled as USE32.
.B \-\-json
keeps JSON on stdout and still writes the UASM file.
MZ and COM only.
NE, .SYS, and a 32-bit extender payload are rejected.
Auto-repack is skipped and the file does not contain a REPACK-V1 block.
COM uses
.B .model tiny
and
.B org 100h
so
.B uasm -bin
matches the COM bytes.
An EXE load image uses
.B org 0
so the
.B uasm -mz
output is that load-image payload only.
It does not rebuild the MZ header.
Restore the header with
.BR bin2exe .
The payload length
.RI ( "e_cparhdr * 16" )
is the full load image.
An image longer than 65536 bytes is split into byte-aligned
.B sN segment
/
.B org 0
/
.B sN ends
chunks of at most 65536 bytes with no padding and no
.BR .stack .
.PP
A multi-segment listing ends with a bare
.B end
so
.B uasm \-bin
accepts it.
The entry label stays inside its
.B sN
segment when the entry is inside the 64 KiB decode window.
An entry past that window is not labeled
.B func_FFFF
and the listing ends with a bare
.BR end .
A same-segment far call is followed when its segment matches the
file CS in the MZ header, including when
.B \-\-base
is left at the default 1000h.
A far call or jump whose segment word is a relocation is followed at
segment*16+offset inside the load image instead.
A target past 64 KiB is not followed.
COM and a one-segment EXE still end with
.B end func_XXXX .
.BR bin2exe (1)
copies the original header, including CS:IP:
.B bin2exe \-\-header ORIGINAL.EXE IMAGE.bin \-o RESTORED.EXE .
The default
.B bin2exe
mode, with no
.BR \-\-header ,
wraps a COM-sized image so DOS enters it at PSP:0100.
dumpexe does not run bin2exe.
.PP
.B \-\-uasm-stats
is enable-only.
It does not turn
.B \-\-uasm
on.
Without
.B \-\-uasm
the program rejects the command.
When both are set and a UASM listing was emitted, one coverage line is
written to stderr (walked image bytes, decoded CFG bytes, bytes emitted as
instructions,
.B db
bytes, and label definitions and whole-token references).
stdout stays the product.
The line is not a JSON field.
There is no disable form.
.PP
An existing default
.I <stem>.asm
or
.I <stem>.repack.exe
is kept.
.B \-o
or
.B \-\-repack-output
may replace the path it names.
An existing
.IR <stem>_UNPACKED.EXE ,
.IR .COM ,
or
.I .asm
is kept.
A kept unpacked image is not paired with a new
.I _UNPACKED.asm .
.PP
.B \-\-no-toolchain
and
.B \-\-json
skip unpack.
There is no
.B \-\-unpack
switch. Auto-repack stays on;
.B \-\-no-repack
turns it off.

.SS CFG, toolchain, and trace options
These flags are parsed by
.BR \-\-help .
Defaults that are already on have only a
.B \-\-no-*
form.
.TP
.B \-\-cfg
Build and print the static CFG with INT and string cross-references.
.TP
.B \-\-cfg-interesting
Print only the interesting-block summary. No full dump.
.TP
.B \-\-cfg-no-insns
CFG edges and tags only. No per-block disassembly.
.TP
.B \-\-cfg-no-calls
Do not follow near call targets as new leaders.
.TP
.BI \-\-cfg-max= N
Maximum basic blocks in the CFG full dump.
Default 500.
The
.B \-d
listing cap is the greater of
.I N
and 2000.
.TP
.BI \-\-cfg-insns= N
Instructions shown per CFG block (default 12; 0 means all).
.TP
.BI \-\-cfg-interesting-max= N
Maximum interesting blocks to expand (default 80).
.TP
.BI \-\-cfg-load-depth= N
Reverse-walk depth for the load and I/O graph (default 6).
.TP
.BI \-\-cfg-load-max= N
Maximum path and FCB seeds in the load graph (default 40).
.TP
.B \-\-no-toolchain
Disable JWASM 1.8, COM-in-EXE, CuteMouse, and packer detection
(default on). Also skips unpack.
.TP
.B \-\-no-dos-extender
Disable DOS extender and DPMI stub detection (default on).
.TP
.BR \-\-bits= 16|32
Force the Capstone x86 width. Default is auto (32 when an extender is detected).
.TP
.BI \-\-map= FILE
Load a symbol map. Disables the need for auto-load.
.TP
.B \-\-no-map
Do not auto-load
.I <stem>.sym
or
.I <stem>.map
(default is auto on).
.TP
.BI \-\-model= M
JWASM export memory model:
tiny, small, medium, compact, large, or huge.
Default small when unknown.
.B .COM
and COM-in-EXE stay tiny.
.TP
.B \-\-no-repack
Do not write
.I <stem>.repack.exe
after a Turbo Pascal or JWASM export (default on).
.TP
.BI \-\-repack-output= PATH
Override the repack EXE path. Implies repack on.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Print every executed instruction. Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .
.TP
.B \-\-sim-quiet
Print only breakpoint hits and a final summary. Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .
.TP
.BI \-\-max-insns= N
Stop after
.I N
instructions (default 64, 10000 with
.BR \-\-trace ,
or 1000000 with
.BR \-\-bp ).
A leading minus is an error.
Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .
.TP
.BI \-\-loop-limit= N
Take a short backward jmp or jcc at most
.I N
times, then fall through
(default 10000; 0 disables). Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .
.TP
.BI \-\-loop-span= XX
Maximum IP distance of a tight loop, in hex (default 100h).
Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .
.TP
.BI \-\-bp= SPEC
Add a breakpoint. Repeatable. Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .
Forms:
.IR ip:XXXX ,
.IR CS:IP ,
.IR int:21 ,
.IR int:21,ah=0F .
.TP
.BI \-\-dump= seg:off:len
Hex-dump memory on each breakpoint hit.
.I seg
is hex or cs, ds, es, or ss.
.I len
is hex and defaults to 40h.
Implies
.BR \-\-simulate .

.SS Simulation Options
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Enable DOS load simulation mode. This performs
.B dynamic analysis
by simulating how DOS loads and starts the program.
The simulator is an in-memory sandbox.
Guest data files start empty.
Guest I/O does not open host files.
A write to handle 1 (stdout) or handle 2 (stderr) returns CF=1 and AX=6.
At most 64 guest files are created.
Truncating a guest file releases its allocation, so recreate does not
keep the old capacity.
Shows:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Initial CPU register state (CS, IP, SS, SP, DS, ES, FLAGS, etc.)
.IP \(bu 2
Relocation fixups applied to the image (MZ EXE only)
.IP \(bu 2
Register tracing — simple instruction emulation showing register changes
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-base= XXXX
Set the load base segment for simulation (hexadecimal value, default: 1000h).
This is the segment address where DOS loads the program.
Common values: 1000, 2000, etc.

.SS COM-Specific Options
These options only affect .COM file analysis; they are silently ignored for MZ EXE and .SYS files.
.TP
.B \-\-psp
Force the .COM file to be treated as containing an embedded PSP.
The entry point will be set to file offset 0100h.
Overrides the automatic PSP detection heuristic.
.TP
.B \-\-no-psp
Force the .COM file to be treated as
.B not
containing an embedded PSP.
The entry point will be set to file offset 0000h.
Overrides the automatic PSP detection heuristic.
These two flags are mutually exclusive.

.SH COM PSP DETECTION
A standard .COM file on disk starts directly with code/data — the PSP is built by DOS in
memory at program load time and is
.B not
part of the file.  Occasionally a .COM is saved as a raw memory snapshot that begins with a
256-byte PSP.
.B dumpexe
distinguishes these cases using two checks:
.IP 1. 4
First two bytes are
.B CDh 20h
(INT 20h instruction — the canonical PSP opener).
.IP 2. 4
The command-tail at file offset
.B 80h
is plausible: the length byte is ≤ 7Eh and the byte at
.B 81h + length
is
.B 0Dh
(carriage return).
.PP
Both checks must pass for a PSP to be declared present.  A file shorter than 256 bytes cannot
contain a PSP and is always treated as no-PSP.
Use
.B \-\-psp
or
.B \-\-no-psp
to override this heuristic.

.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.SS Static Header Information
Always displayed first.
.B MZ EXE:
comprehensive header analysis including DOS File Size, Load Image Size,
Relocation Table entry count and address, Header Size, Memory Requirements,
Entry Point locations (file offset and image offset), and initial SS:SP / CS:IP values.
.br
.B COM:
File Size, Load Model (PSP embedded or not), Entry Point File Offset, and
initial CS:IP / SS:SP / DS / ES values.
.br
.B SYS:
Driver type (character or block), attributes, strategy and interrupt entry
point offsets, device name (char) or unit count and signature (block), and file size.

.SS Relocation Table (-r, MZ EXE only)
Formatted table showing each relocation entry with:
.IP \(bu 2
Entry number
.IP \(bu 2
Segment:Offset pair
.IP \(bu 2
File location in hex
.IP \(bu 2
Linear offset within image
.PP
Also shows any padding between header and relocation table, and between table and image.

.SS Hex+ASCII Dump (-x)
Complete dump from entry point to EOF in canonical hexdump -C format:
.IP \(bu 2
8-digit hex addresses (lowercase)
.IP \(bu 2
16 bytes per line with space after 8th byte
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII panel with | delimiters
.IP \(bu 2
Zero-compression: repeated identical lines shown as single * (saves enormous space)
.PP
Example:
.nf
00000420  1e b8 00 00 50 06 2e a1  00 00 8e c0 8e d8 58 a3  |....P.........X.|
00000430  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  |................|
*
00000450  ff ff ff ff 21 00 2a 00  33 00 44 00 55 00 66 00  |....!.*.3.D.U.f.|
.fi

.SS Disassembly (-d)
.B Static code analysis
using Capstone x86-16 disassembler. Shows file offset, raw bytes, mnemonic, and
operands for instructions reached from the entry point.
A NE segment stops at its on-disk end.
INT 20h, INT 27h, and INT 21h with AH 00h, 4Ch, or 31h end the walk.
Opcode 98 is shown as
.B cbw
and opcode 99 as
.B cwd
unless a 66h prefix is present.
INT instructions
are annotated with descriptions from the Ralf Brown Interrupt List (RBIL) database.

.SS Simulation (--simulate)
.B Dynamic execution trace
that simulates DOS loading and program startup.
A direct far call or jump sets CS:IP from the segment and the offset.
Opcode 98 is CBW in this 16-bit guest (Capstone names it CWDE).
Guest data files start empty.
Create, open, read, and write stay inside the simulator and do not
open host files.
A write to handle 1 or handle 2 returns CF=1 and AX=6.
At most 64 guest files. Truncate releases capacity:

.B Initial Register State:
.RS
For MZ EXE: CS and SS are shifted by the load base; DS and ES = load base.
.br
For .COM: CS = DS = ES = SS = load base; IP = 0100h; SP = FFFEh.
.RE

.B Relocation Fixups (MZ EXE only):
.RS
Shows each segment value being adjusted by the load base.
.RE

.B Register Tracing (with Capstone):
.RS
Best-effort emulation of first ~20 instructions showing register changes.
Not a full emulator — focuses on common register operations for quick insight.
.RE

.SH STATIC vs DYNAMIC ANALYSIS
.B Static Analysis (-d):
.RS
Disassembles all code without execution. Shows complete instruction stream. Good for
understanding program structure, finding all code paths, and analyzing algorithms.
.RE

.B Dynamic Analysis (--simulate):
.RS
Simulates execution from entry point. Shows register state changes. Good for
understanding program initialization and verifying relocation correctness.
.RE

.B Combined (-d --simulate):
.RS
Shows both full disassembly and dynamic trace — complete picture of the binary.
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error (invalid file, bad arguments, corrupted header)

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dumpexe program.exe
Show only static header information (MZ EXE)
.TP
.B dumpexe -r program.exe
Show header and relocation table
.TP
.B dumpexe -x game.exe
Show header and complete hex dump
.TP
.B dumpexe -d game.exe
Show header and full disassembly (static analysis)
.TP
.B dumpexe --simulate game.exe
Show header and DOS load simulation (dynamic analysis)
.TP
.B dumpexe -a program.exe
Show everything: relocations, hexdump, disassembly
.TP
.B dumpexe -d --simulate --base=2000 game.exe
Disassemble and simulate loading at segment 2000h
.TP
.B dumpexe program.com
Analyze a .COM file (PSP presence auto-detected)
.TP
.B dumpexe --no-psp -d program.com
Analyze a .COM file, forcing no-PSP mode (entry at file offset 0)
.TP
.B dumpexe --psp -d memdump.com
Analyze a .COM memory dump that includes a PSP (entry at file offset 0100h)
.TP
.B dumpexe driver.sys
Analyze a DOS device driver
.TP
.B dumpexe -x program.com | less
Page through hexdump output of a .COM file

.SH FILES
.TP
.B dumpexe.h
Umbrella header that includes all modular sub-headers
.TP
.B com.h
MS-DOS .COM format constants (PSP size, entry offset, command-tail offsets)
.TP
.B com_analysis.h
COM analysis functions: PSP heuristic detection and full analysis output
.TP
.B dumpexe.cpp
Main implementation file

.SH TECHNICAL DETAILS
.SS MZ EXE Format
.IP \(bu 2
Signature: 'MZ' (0x5A4D little-endian)
.IP \(bu 2
28-byte minimum header
.IP \(bu 2
Header sizes in paragraphs (16-byte units)
.IP \(bu 2
File size: num_blocks × 512 when final_len is 0 (last page full); otherwise ((num_blocks-1) × 512) + final_len
.IP \(bu 2
Entry point: header_size + (CS × 16) + IP

.SS COM Format
.IP \(bu 2
Flat binary image; DOS places the PSP at CS:0000 and loads the file at CS:0100
.IP \(bu 2
CS = DS = ES = SS = load segment at startup
.IP \(bu 2
SP initialised to 0xFFFE (top of 64 KB segment minus two bytes)
.IP \(bu 2
Execution always starts at CS:0100h

.SS SYS Device Driver Format
.IP \(bu 2
Starts with 0xFFFFFFFF (next-driver pointer = end of chain)
.IP \(bu 2
16-bit attribute word (bit 15 = character device)
.IP \(bu 2
Strategy and interrupt handler offsets
.IP \(bu 2
8-byte device name (character) or unit count + 7-byte signature (block)

.SS Register State Representation
Uses packed unions and bitfields for authentic x86 register access:
.IP \(bu 2
AX/BX/CX/DX with hi/lo byte access (AH/AL, etc.)
.IP \(bu 2
Segment registers: CS, DS, ES, SS
.IP \(bu 2
Pointers: IP, SP, BP, SI, DI
.IP \(bu 2
FLAGS with individual bit access (CF, ZF, SF, OF, etc.)

.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Read-only analysis — never modifies files
.IP \(bu 2
Works cross-platform (analyze DOS binaries on Linux/Unix)
.IP \(bu 2
Zero-compression in hex dumps saves space for AI context windows
.IP \(bu 2
Hex values displayed lowercase (canonical format)
.IP \(bu 2
Simulation is best-effort, not cycle-accurate emulation
.IP \(bu 2
Requires GCC 14+ and libcapstone-dev to build

